1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,478
Last time, your three sides were just there.

2
00:00:04,378 --> 00:00:07,422
They met every crew the same way,

3
00:00:07,872 --> 00:00:10,046
whatever that crew had done.

4
00:00:11,846 --> 00:00:14,020
Today they start to remember.

5
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:17,528
Each side gets something it values.

6
00:00:17,978 --> 00:00:21,457
The crew earns a name with each one,

7
00:00:21,907 --> 00:00:26,254
by what they say and by the work they finish.

8
00:00:27,154 --> 00:00:30,198
And that name changes prices, and jobs,

9
00:00:30,648 --> 00:00:33,257
and how a station says hello.

10
00:00:34,457 --> 00:00:38,804
The game measures a captain on seven pairs of traits.

11
00:00:39,704 --> 00:00:42,748
There's honest or liar, fearsome or cowardly,

12
00:00:43,198 --> 00:00:45,807
generous or selfish, and four more.

13
00:00:47,607 --> 00:00:50,215
Neither end is the good one.

14
00:00:51,115 --> 00:00:55,028
A navy can value doing it by the book,

15
00:00:55,478 --> 00:00:59,391
and a gang of wreckers can value being feared.

16
00:01:01,191 --> 00:01:04,235
So each side gets one line, Values,

17
00:01:04,685 --> 00:01:09,467
with the traits it cares about, and a weight on each.

18
00:01:10,367 --> 00:01:12,976
The farmers want honest and generous.

19
00:01:13,876 --> 00:01:16,920
The miners want it by the book.

20
00:01:17,820 --> 00:01:19,993
And the wreckers want fearsome.

21
00:01:21,193 --> 00:01:26,846
From those, the game works out one number for each side, called standing.

22
00:01:27,746 --> 00:01:29,485
It starts at zero,

23
00:01:29,935 --> 00:01:33,848
and it's an average across what that side values.

24
00:01:35,648 --> 00:01:37,822
Look at what that means.

25
00:01:38,722 --> 00:01:43,504
Twenty in both of Hollin's values is a standing of twenty.

26
00:01:44,404 --> 00:01:49,187
But honesty alone has to reach thirty-five to do the same.

27
00:01:50,087 --> 00:01:54,435
And honesty alone, pushed all the way, stops at fifty-seven.

28
00:01:56,235 --> 00:02:02,322
So a crew can't win a side over by being good at one thing.

29
00:02:03,222 --> 00:02:08,004
They have to be the whole of what that side admires.

30
00:02:09,204 --> 00:02:11,813
How does the crew earn it?

31
00:02:12,263 --> 00:02:16,176
There are two ways, and the first is work.

32
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:20,120
When a ship finishes a side's job,

33
00:02:20,570 --> 00:02:24,483
its standing with that side goes up by five.

34
00:02:25,383 --> 00:02:27,557
I write nothing for that.

35
00:02:28,007 --> 00:02:30,180
It comes with the job.

36
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:35,459
But the template's jobs don't have endings yet,

37
00:02:35,909 --> 00:02:38,517
so I give the escort one:

38
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:42,446
done when the ship reaches the miners' home.

39
00:02:43,346 --> 00:02:47,259
And I give the patrol a rank, tier two.

40
00:02:48,159 --> 00:02:51,202
A side only offers tier two work

41
00:02:51,652 --> 00:02:54,696
to a ship at twenty or more.

42
00:02:55,896 --> 00:02:59,374
The second way is what the crew says.

43
00:03:00,274 --> 00:03:02,013
This is a call,

44
00:03:02,463 --> 00:03:06,811
and if you've taken Class 2 you know its shape.

45
00:03:07,711 --> 00:03:11,189
If you haven't, this is all of it.

46
00:03:12,989 --> 00:03:14,728
Speaker is the side.

47
00:03:15,628 --> 00:03:20,846
When comms means this opens when the crew hails that side's station.

48
00:03:21,746 --> 00:03:26,963
A line starting with a percent sign is something the station says.

49
00:03:27,863 --> 00:03:33,950
And a line starting with a hyphen is an answer, which becomes a button.

50
00:03:35,750 --> 00:03:39,228
Now the two parts that are about today.

51
00:03:40,128 --> 00:03:43,607
After the semicolon is what the answer does:

52
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:46,230
it costs a hundred credits,

53
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:51,028
and it earns twenty honest and twenty generous with Hollin.

54
00:03:51,928 --> 00:03:54,102
That answer is the deed.

55
00:03:55,902 --> 00:03:59,815
And up here, in curly brackets, is a guard.

56
00:04:00,715 --> 00:04:05,933
This line is only said to a ship at twenty or more.

57
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:09,876
So the station greets a friend differently,

58
00:04:10,326 --> 00:04:14,674
and that's how the crew finds out where they stand.

59
00:04:15,874 --> 00:04:19,787
Two things to know before you write your own.

60
00:04:21,587 --> 00:04:24,196
First, guard every line, or none.

61
00:04:25,096 --> 00:04:29,009
A line with no guard can always be said,

62
00:04:29,459 --> 00:04:33,372
so a friend would hear either one, at random.

63
00:04:35,172 --> 00:04:38,215
Second, an answer can be given again.

64
00:04:39,115 --> 00:04:43,028
The crew can hail as often as they like,

65
00:04:43,478 --> 00:04:46,087
and the deed happens every time.

66
00:04:46,987 --> 00:04:49,596
That's why the levy costs money.

67
00:04:51,396 --> 00:04:56,613
Here's the wreckers' call, and I've left the cost off on purpose.

68
00:04:57,513 --> 00:05:00,991
Watch what that does when I play it.

69
00:05:02,191 --> 00:05:05,670
Standing only matters where it crosses a line.

70
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:09,613
At twenty, a side offers better work,

71
00:05:10,063 --> 00:05:13,976
and an enemy will deal with you at all.

72
00:05:14,876 --> 00:05:19,224
Every point above zero adds one percent to the pay.

73
00:05:20,124 --> 00:05:24,907
A ceasefire costs twenty credits for each point short of thirty.

74
00:05:25,357 --> 00:05:30,574
So it's six hundred to a stranger, and free to a friend.

75
00:05:32,374 --> 00:05:35,852
You can move every one of those lines,

76
00:05:36,302 --> 00:05:38,911
and the page shows you how.

77
00:05:39,811 --> 00:05:42,420
I'm leaving them where they are,

78
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:44,609
and lint says clean.

79
00:05:45,809 --> 00:05:51,026
Here's Hollin Compact, with an escort for two fifty, and no patrol.

80
00:05:52,826 --> 00:05:58,478
I hail them, and I get the cold line, the one for strangers.

81
00:05:59,378 --> 00:06:01,117
I pay the levy.

82
00:06:02,917 --> 00:06:05,526
Now look at the station again.

83
00:06:06,426 --> 00:06:10,339
There's the patrol, and the escort pays three hundred.

84
00:06:11,239 --> 00:06:16,022
I hail once more, and this time they know my ship.

85
00:06:17,822 --> 00:06:19,996
So I take the escort,

86
00:06:20,446 --> 00:06:23,054
and on Helm I engage it.

87
00:06:23,954 --> 00:06:25,693
It's done on arrival,

88
00:06:26,143 --> 00:06:28,752
and that's five more with Hollin.

89
00:06:29,952 --> 00:06:31,691
Now for the Gleaners.

90
00:06:32,591 --> 00:06:34,765
The ceasefire is on offer,

91
00:06:35,215 --> 00:06:38,259
and it would cost me six hundred.

92
00:06:39,159 --> 00:06:40,898
I don't press it.

93
00:06:42,698 --> 00:06:44,437
I hail them instead,

94
00:06:44,887 --> 00:06:48,800
and I tell them I go where I like.

95
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:53,613
That's one bold answer, and my standing is twenty.

96
00:06:55,413 --> 00:06:57,587
Look at the station now.

97
00:06:58,487 --> 00:07:00,226
They'll give me work,

98
00:07:00,676 --> 00:07:03,285
and the ceasefire costs two hundred,

99
00:07:03,735 --> 00:07:05,474
so I take it.

100
00:07:07,274 --> 00:07:09,883
And now the trap I left.

101
00:07:10,783 --> 00:07:14,696
I give the same answer twice more, for nothing,

102
00:07:15,146 --> 00:07:19,928
and I'm at fifty-seven with people who were shooting at me.

103
00:07:20,828 --> 00:07:25,176
It stops there, because they value something else as well.

104
00:07:26,076 --> 00:07:30,424
But that's what a free deed does to a story.

105
00:07:31,624 --> 00:07:33,363
Now it's your turn.

106
00:07:34,263 --> 00:07:38,176
Give each of your sides two or three values,

107
00:07:38,626 --> 00:07:41,670
and say out loud why they'd care.

108
00:07:42,570 --> 00:07:46,917
Rewrite both calls in their voices, and keep the guards.

109
00:07:47,817 --> 00:07:49,557
And fix my trap:

110
00:07:50,007 --> 00:07:55,224
make being feared by one side cost the crew something with another.

111
00:07:57,024 --> 00:07:59,633
Next time, we draw the map.
